# When to Write an ADR

Ask: if this decision changed later, would other components, deployed devices or
stored data break? Choose the first tier that fits.

## 1. Full ADR

- Breaking changes to the API contract or schema
- Breaking changes to MQTT topics or payloads
- Modbus or RS-485 register maps and hardware communication
- Stored event or other persisted formats
- Security boundaries: authentication, authorization, secrets, TLS
- New infrastructure, hosting or runtime dependencies

Write it even if nobody asked for documentation, and open it as its own PR before
the implementation.

## 2. Plan section in the PR description

Design with real trade-offs that stays inside one component: internal structure,
test tooling, build or CI tuning, refactors. State the choice and the alternatives
you rejected.

## 3. Nothing

No real trade-off: bug fixes, dependency bumps, small features following an
existing pattern.

If unsure whether a change needs a Full ADR, write the Full ADR.
If unsure between the Plan section and Nothing, pick the lower one and say so in the PR.

## Working with existing ADRs

- Check whether an ADR already covers the decision.
- If it is outdated, edit it while it is editable; otherwise supersede it. See
  `adr-format-and-lifecycle.mdc`.
- Reference the ADR path in the PR description.

If running in ask/read-only mode, draft ADR content in the response and ask to
switch to agent mode for file creation.
